export default {"builtAt":"2026-11-04T06:30:10.758Z","files":{"01-the-bet/diagnostic.md":"# Three-Axis Vulnerability Diagnostic\n\n> Week 1 · Oct 26, 2026 · Draft for partner challenge\n\n## Product\n\n**Product:** Leave Ledger. California leave of absence management for employers with 5 to 200 employees. It works out which state and federal leaves an employee qualifies for, what the state pays, and the notices and deadlines the employer owes, then gives HR, the employee and the manager each their own view.\n**Your Role:** Founder and product lead (solo build, test site on Netlify with 10 test accounts).\n\n**Scoring convention used here:** Contextual Moat and Data Advantage, 5 = strongest position. Platform Exposure, 5 = most exposed.\n\n---\n\n## The market in numbers\n\n| Segment | California businesses | Why it matters |\n|---|---|---|\n| 5 to 49 employees | 328,201 | Owe CFRA, PDL, bereavement and reproductive loss leave, but not federal FMLA. Most leave tools are built around FMLA. |\n| 50 to 249 employees | 48,240 | Owe all of the above plus FMLA. Where mid-market vendors start. |\n| **5 to 249 total** | **376,441** | Rough employee base: about 9.8M (midpoint estimate per size band) |\n\nSource: EDD size-of-business table, third quarter (statewide row). The employee figure multiplies each band's count by its midpoint, so treat it as an order of magnitude, not a fact.\n\n**Price anchor:** Tilt lists $4 to $6 per employee per month (G2, Dec 2025). At that price, 9.8M employees is a $470M to $700M a year ceiling. Real reachable revenue is a small fraction of that; M3 works the actual number.\n\n---\n\n## Scores\n\n### Contextual Moat · 2/5\n*Workflow depth × switching cost. Would users leave in a weekend if a competitor showed up?*\n\n**Score rationale:** The workflow is real: a deadline clock that starts when a manager hears about a leave, stacked entitlements (PDL then CFRA bonding, with SDI and PFL pay), notices, and three role views with server-side privacy. But today Leave Ledger holds no history and no documents, and it does not touch payroll. A user could move in a weekend. Switching cost appears once it holds the 12-month rolling CFRA history, signed notices and certifications for every employee, because that record is what an employer needs in a dispute.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **Tilt** (formerly Sparrow). $4 to $6 per employee per month, two-way Gusto sync, all leave types nationwide. If Tilt adds a small-business tier, it arrives with payroll integration on day one, which Leave Ledger lacks.\n\n---\n\n### Data Advantage · 1/5\n*Proprietary signal that compounds with usage. What do you see that OpenAI doesn't?*\n\n**Score rationale:** No users yet, so no data. The rules engine encodes public law that anyone can read. The signal that could compound:\n1. How employees and managers actually describe a need for time off (\"my mom's surgery is next month\"), mapped to the leave type HR confirmed. This trains intake.\n2. Outcomes per California leave pattern: real durations by reason, how often PDL runs into bonding, how often certifications come back late.\n3. California edge cases HR had to override, which become rules or tests.\n\nNone of this exists until roughly 50 active employers are using it.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **ChatGPT and other general assistants.** An office manager can already ask \"my employee is pregnant, what leave do they get in California?\" and get a decent answer for free. What the assistant lacks is the employee's hours, tenure, prior leave and the deadline clock. That is the gap to own.\n\n---\n\n### Platform Exposure · 4/5\n*Encroachment risk × pivot speed. If Apple/Google/OpenAI ships your hero feature native, then what?*\n\n**Score rationale:** For this product, the platforms are the payroll and HR systems small businesses already pay for. Encroachment is already happening, one tier up:\n- **Paylocity** acquired Aidora, an AI-native leave management company, on July 9, 2026. Aimed at mid-market and enterprise.\n- **Mitratech** launched Mitratech Leave, AI-native leave compliance, at SHRM26 in June 2026. Aimed at mid-sized and multi-state employers.\n- **Gusto, Rippling and Justworks** are where California small businesses run payroll. Any of them can bundle leave tracking at no extra price.\n\nPivot speed is the one strength: the codebase is small and could become an add-on inside a payroll marketplace within weeks.\n\n**Named attacker (draft, for partner challenge):** **Gusto.** It already holds the hours, hire dates and pay that eligibility depends on. A California leave module bundled into its plans would remove the reason to buy a separate tool.\n\n---\n\n## Top Vulnerability\n\nDistribution. Small businesses buy HR software through their payroll provider. If Gusto or Rippling ships California-specific leave (CFRA and PDL stacking plus a notice clock) before Leave Ledger has paying users, a standalone product has no way in.\n\n## Confidence Level\n\n**L (low).** The product logic is sound and the gap for 5 to 49 employee employers is real on paper, but nobody outside this build has used it or said they would pay. Moves to M after 10 interviews with California office managers or HR generalists.\n","01-the-bet/prototype.md":"# The Prototype Bet\n\n> Week 1 · Oct 26, 2026\n\n## What I Built\n\nA working test site where an HR admin, a manager and an employee each see a California leave from their side. The HR admin gets stacked entitlements, a state pay estimate, the notices owed, and a deadline clock. The employee gets their balances and next steps. The manager gets dates only.\n\n## Tool Used\n\nClaude (product, rules research, code), GitHub (`francis4531/leave-ledger`), Netlify (hosting, serverless API, Blobs storage). Claude commits directly to the repo; no local editor needed.\n\n## Prototype Link\n\nhttps://spectacular-mousse-c413d6.netlify.app · 10 test accounts, shared password from Netlify settings.\n\nWhat works today:\n- **Rules engine** for CFRA, PDL, FMLA, SDI, PFL, paid sick leave, bereavement and reproductive loss, checked against sources in October 2026.\n- **Deadline clock**: 5 business days for the first notice, 15 calendar days for certification, 5 business days to designate. It skips weekends and company holidays.\n- **Server-side privacy**: managers never receive the reason for a leave, and employees only receive their own records.\n- **Manager flag**: when a manager hears about a possible leave, the notice clock starts.\n- **Clerk intake (added Oct 27)**: an employee describes the need in their own words (English or Spanish) and Claude Haiku 5.5 fills in the request form, marking anything it is unsure of. HR sees the original words next to what Clerk read and what the employee changed. HR can also ask it to read a manager's note. The rules engine still decides eligibility. Every intake is logged with its corrections, and a 20-case golden set (`tests/intake-golden.json`, 4 adversarial) with `scripts/eval-intake.mjs` measures accuracy.\n\n## AI Value Archetype\n\n**Copilot** (confirmed Oct 26, 2026). The AI inside the product is named **Clerk** (Oct 29, 2026): it prepares the paperwork, and HR decides.\n\nThe prototype is a deterministic rules engine with the first Clerk piece (intake) on top of it. The AI layer sits on top of the rules engine, and the rules engine stays the only thing that decides eligibility:\n\n1. **Intake.** An employee or manager writes in plain words (\"my dad has surgery on the 12th, I need a few weeks\"). Clerk turns that into a draft case: likely leave type, family relation, dates. HR confirms it with one click.\n2. **Plain-language answers.** Employees ask \"will I get paid?\" and get an answer grounded in their own case, in English or Spanish.\n3. **Notice drafting.** Clerk fills in the eligibility, designation and return-to-work notices from the case for HR to review and send.\n\nClerk never decides eligibility or denies a leave; a human signs off on every notice. That boundary is the trust story for Module 4.\n\nWhy not the other archetypes: an **Automator** that sends notices on its own is too risky for legal notices. An **Oracle** with no workflow is what ChatGPT already gives away for free.\n\n## The Bet in One Sentence\n\nCalifornia employers with 5 to 200 employees get every leave right the first time: Leave Ledger turns \"I need time off\" into the right state and federal leaves, pay and deadlines, so a part-time office manager does not need an employment lawyer or a leave vendor built for 5,000-person companies.\n\n**Why now:**\n- CFRA reached employers with 5+ employees in 2021.\n- California keeps adding leave rules: bereavement (2023), reproductive loss and 40 hours of sick leave (2024), and PFL for a designated individual from July 2028.\n- The AI-native leave tools that launched in 2026 (Aidora, now part of Paylocity; Mitratech Leave) are built for mid-market and enterprise.\n\n## Kill Criteria\n\nStop, or pivot to an add-on inside a payroll platform, if any of these happen:\n\n| # | Evidence that kills or changes the bet | Check by |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Fewer than 3 of 10 interviewed California office managers or HR generalists handled a CFRA or PDL leave in the last 12 months and called it painful | Nov 23, 2026 |\n| 2 | Fewer than 2 of those 10 would pay at least $3 per employee per month (our price; was $4, Tilt's lowest tier when first set, changed Nov 3) or a flat monthly price of similar size | Nov 23, 2026 |\n| 3 | Gusto or Rippling ships California-specific leave stacking with a notice clock before Leave Ledger has 5 paying employers | Ongoing; review monthly |\n| 4 | AI intake classifies under 90% of a 50-case golden set into the right leave type | Built and tested in Module 4 |\n\nIf #4 fails, the bet survives as a rules-and-workflow product with no AI in the decision path, and the archetype is revisited.\n","02-the-moat/data-flywheel.md":"# Data Flywheel Map\n\n> Week 2 · Oct 28, 2026 · Draft for partner challenge\n> Score each loop 1-5. Your weakest loop is where competitors attack first.\n\n**Starting point:** Leave Ledger is a test build with 10 test accounts and no real customers. Clerk intake (built Oct 27) is the first part that produces data that could compound. Scores below describe what exists today, not the plan.\n\n## Flywheel Loops\n\n| Loop | What It Measures | Score 1 | Score 5 | Score |\n|------|------------------|---------|---------|-------|\n| **Correction** | Do users fix AI outputs? Is that signal captured and reused? | No capture | Automated retraining | 2/5 |\n| **Preference** | Does the product learn individual / team preferences over time? | Stateless | Deep personalization | 1/5 |\n| **Domain Context** | Does usage in one area improve quality in adjacent areas? | Siloed | Cross-domain transfer | 2/5 |\n| **Network** | Does each new user / team make the product better for everyone? | Isolated | Strong network effects | 1/5 |\n\n### Correction Loop · 2/5\n**What you capture today:** Every Clerk intake is stored with the user's own words, what Clerk read (leave type, family member, start date, length, confidence per field) and which fields the user changed before sending. HR sees \"Sent as Clerk read it\" or \"Changed before sending: start date\". HR Settings shows the share sent without changes and the count of fixes per field.\n**How it compounds:** It doesn't yet. Corrections are captured but nothing feeds them back. Step to 3/5: a weekly review that turns each fixed intake into a row in `tests/intake-golden.json`, and the most common misses into prompt examples, with the eval score tracked over time. Step to 4/5: per-company examples (\"at this company, 'a few weeks' usually means 4\").\n\n### Preference Loop · 1/5\n**What you capture today:** Nothing per company or per user beyond settings (headcount, holidays). The product does not know whether a company pays for bereavement, tops up state pay, or lets employees use vacation first.\n**How it compounds:** A company policy profile, learned from HR's edits and set once in Settings, would change the plan and the notices for every future leave at that company. Employee language preference (Spanish) would carry into answers and notices. Each is small, but together they make switching costly: a new tool starts without them.\n\n### Domain Context Loop · 2/5\n**What you capture today:** One case feeds the next for the same employee. CFRA weeks used in the rolling 12 months reduce what is left, sick hours carry across requests, and the manager's flag becomes the HR case. The rules engine is shared across all 8 leave types, so a rule fix (for example, holidays in the deadline count) improves every leave.\n**How it compounds:** Within a company, a full history makes each new decision more accurate (no double-counting of CFRA, no missed PDL time). This is the start of switching cost: a competitor would not have the 12-month record that a dispute depends on.\n\n### Network Loop · 1/5\n**What you capture today:** Nothing. Each company is isolated, which is correct for health-related data.\n**How it compounds:** Only through de-identified, aggregated benchmarks shown to HR: typical length by leave type, how often certifications come back late, how often PDL runs into bonding. Two limits: it needs enough companies to be meaningful and safe (for example, no figure shown unless it pools at least 10 companies), and leave reasons are sensitive health information, so it must be opt-in and never expose an individual. A second network effect is a shared library of California edge cases (rules HR had to override), which helps every customer and holds no personal data.\n\n**Total Flywheel Score: 6/20**\n**Weakest Loop:** Network and Preference (1/5 each). Network is the one a competitor with scale attacks first: Tilt, Gusto or Paylocity see far more leaves than Leave Ledger will for years.\n**Fix for weakest loop:** Do not try to win on network effects. Invest in Correction and Preference, which work with a single customer, and build the de-identified California benchmark only after 25 or more active companies. The near-term fix is the Correction loop (weekly golden-set review), because it costs nothing and its score is already measured.\n\n---\n\n## Competitive Position\n\nTwo axes that matter to a 5 to 49 employee California employer:\n\n- **California depth** (horizontal): from generic multi-state leave tracking to full California stacking (PDL then CFRA bonding, SDI and PFL pay, bereavement, reproductive loss, notice clock).\n- **Fit for a small business** (vertical): from enterprise sales and implementation to self-serve setup at a price a 20-person company accepts.\n\n| Player | California depth | Small-business fit | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Paylocity + Aidora | High | Low | AI-native leave, acquired Jul 2026; aimed at mid-market and enterprise |\n| Mitratech Leave | High | Low | AI-native leave compliance, launched Jun 2026; mid-sized and multi-state |\n| Tilt | Medium to high | Medium | $4 to $6 per employee per month, in-house leave experts, Gusto and Rippling integrations, AI assistant \"Tilley\" with English and Spanish phone intake (premium support customers) |\n| Rippling Leave Management | Medium | Medium | Built-in leave app; Rippling suggests adding Tilt for complex cases |\n| Gusto | Low | High | PTO requests, accruals and balances inside payroll; no sign of CFRA eligibility, notices or state leave stacking |\n| CalChamber | High (content) | High | Forms, guides and a helpline, not a workflow |\n| ChatGPT and similar | Medium | High | Free answers; no employee data, no deadlines, no record |\n| **Leave Ledger** | **High** | **High (target)** | Rules engine, notice clock, three role views, Clerk intake; no integrations, no customers |\n\n**Placement:** Leave Ledger aims for the top right corner, where only CalChamber (content, not software) and Tilt (partly) sit today. That corner is attractive and also the easiest to copy, because the rules are public law.\n\nSources: [Tilt's Tilley assistant](https://hellotilt.com/platform/tilley/), [Rippling and Tilt](https://www.rippling.com/blog/tilt-rippling-the-easy-way-to-automate-leave-management), [Gusto time off, per LeaveBalance (a competitor), Jul 2026](https://www.leavebalance.com/blog/gusto-time-off-tracking/), [Tilt pricing on G2](https://www.g2.com/products/tilt-tilt/pricing). M1 sources cover Paylocity and Mitratech.\n\n---\n\n## Encroachment Threat Assessment\n\nTime-to-threat and value-at-risk figures are estimates for partner challenge, not measurements.\n\n### 1. Platform Encroachment\n**Attacker:** Gusto (where California small businesses already run payroll).\n**Vector:** Adds a California leave module to its plans: CFRA and PDL eligibility from the hours and hire dates it already holds, a notice checklist, and payroll pay codes that update automatically.\n**Time-to-threat:** 6 to 18 months. Gusto has the data and the customers; nothing public suggests it is building this now.\n**% of value at risk:** About 70%. Eligibility, balances and tracking would be covered. What it would likely not match early: the notice clock tied to the manager flag, PDL to CFRA stacking detail, and Clerk.\n\n### 2. Vertical Competitor\n**Attacker:** Tilt.\n**Vector:** A lower-priced, self-serve tier for small employers, using its existing Gusto integration and Tilley assistant.\n**Time-to-threat:** 3 to 12 months. It already has the product, the AI intake and the integrations; the only gap is packaging for very small companies.\n**% of value at risk:** About 80%. Tilt's main weakness here is price and a sales-led model built for larger companies.\n\n### 3. Adjacent Expansion\n**Attacker:** CalChamber.\n**Vector:** Turns its leave content, forms and helpline into a guided workflow for its existing California members.\n**Time-to-threat:** 12 to 24 months. Its strength is trust and distribution, not software.\n**% of value at risk:** About 40%, mostly the \"help me get this right\" value for 5 to 49 employee members.\n\n---\n\n## 90-Day Encroachment Plan\n\n*Draft attack for your partner to play and sharpen.*\n\n**Attacker:** Gusto.\n**Attack vector (target the weakest loop):** Network. Gusto sees leave patterns across hundreds of thousands of small businesses. It ships benchmarks and defaults (\"most California companies your size give 5 paid bereavement days\") that Leave Ledger cannot match.\n**Weeks 1-4 - what they ship:** \"California leave\" inside Gusto: an eligibility check (CFRA, PDL) from payroll data, a leave request type, and a notice checklist, free in existing plans.\n**Weeks 5-8 - how they poach users:** An in-app banner for every California customer with 5 or more employees: \"You owe CFRA leave. We now track it for you.\" One-click setup, no new vendor, no new login, no per-employee fee.\n**Weeks 9-12 - why users don't come back:** Leave pay codes, balances and history now live in payroll. Leaving Gusto's module means re-entering history and losing automatic pay updates.\n**Your defense:**\n1. **Be inside Gusto, not against it.** List in Gusto's app directory with a two-way sync (hours, hire dates, pay codes). Position as the California leave specialist that Gusto customers add, the way Tilt already does.\n2. **Own the record.** The notice clock, the signed notices and the 12-month leave history are what an employer needs in a dispute. Make exporting it easy, and make keeping it in Leave Ledger the safer choice.\n3. **Win on correctness for California.** Publish the rules engine's test cases (PDL to bonding, bereavement within 3 months, reproductive loss caps) and Clerk's accuracy score. A free bundled checklist does not ship with either.\n4. **Go through advisors.** Payroll bookkeepers, PEOs and employment lawyers who serve 5 to 49 employee companies are a channel Gusto does not control.\n","02-the-moat/kill-switch.md":"# Kill Switch Audit\n\n> Week 2 · Oct 28, 2026 · Draft for partner challenge\n\n**Where AI is used today:** only Clerk intake (`netlify/lib/intake.mjs`). One model call per request, about 1,500 tokens in and 200 out. Eligibility, pay estimates and deadlines run in the rules engine with no AI. If the AI is off or failing, users fill in the form by hand and nothing else changes.\n\n## Vendor Dependency Assessment\n\n| Dimension | Current State | Risk Level | 48-Hour Action |\n|-----------|--------------|------------|---------------|\n| **Provider** | One provider: Anthropic, Claude Haiku 5.5, called directly over its API. Model name set by `LL_MODEL`. | M | Run the 20-case golden set on a second provider's small model and record the score. |\n| **Abstraction** | Partial. The prompt and the output contract (a JSON draft validated field by field) are provider-neutral, but the HTTP call is written for Anthropic's API. | L | Move the call behind a small adapter (`callModel(system, message)`) with Anthropic and one other provider. |\n| **Routing** | None. Every request goes to one model. There is no fallback model, only the manual form. | M | Add a fallback: if the main model errors or times out, retry once on the second provider; if that fails, show the form. |\n| **Eval** | A 50-case golden set with tricky (adversarial) cases, run from the admin inbox against the live model, plus a live \"sent without changes\" rate in HR Settings. First valid run Nov 3: 100% on leave type (see below). | L | Add 10 to 20 real phrasings from interviews to the golden set, then re-run it on every model change. |\n\n## Portability Score\n\n**Partial.** The parts that make the product valuable (rules engine, deadline clock, role views, the intake output contract) do not depend on any AI vendor. Switching models means changing one file and re-running the golden set. It is not \"Ready\" until a second provider is wired in and scored.\n\n## First measured numbers (Nov 3, 2026)\n\nThese replace the Week 2 token estimate and set the Week 4 baseline.\n\n| Measure | Result | Target |\n|---|---|---|\n| Leave type right | **100%** of 50 cases | 90% (kill criterion) |\n| Every field right | 100% | |\n| Tricky requests handled | 100% | |\n| Average reply time | 2.3 seconds | |\n| Errors | 0 | |\n| Cost of a full 50-case run | about one cent | |\n\nModel: Claude Haiku 5.5. Run from the admin inbox, Nov 3, 10:27 PM PT.\n\n**Excluded:** an earlier run the same evening (8:54 PM) failed all 50 cases because the API key in Netlify was invalid (`401 authentication_error`). That measured the key, not Clerk. The key was replaced, and the app now trims stray spaces and line breaks from its settings and shows the AI provider's last error in the inbox.\n\n**Caveat:** the 50 cases were written alongside the prompt, so 100% is the ceiling, not the real-world rate. The real baseline is how often HR or the employee changes Clerk's draft before sending (\"Sent without changes\" in HR Settings), which starts counting with design partners. Requests and AI cost this month in HR Settings are near zero until real use begins.\n\n## If Anthropic doubles pricing tomorrow:\n\nLittle changes. At today's list price (Haiku 5.5: $0.10 per million input tokens, $0.50 per million output) one intake costs about $0.00025. Doubled, about $0.0005. A 20-employee company with 10 leaves a year would spend under one cent a year on intake. The 48-hour response is to do nothing on cost, and use the moment to finish the adapter and second-provider eval above.\n\nSource: [Claude Haiku 5.5 overview and pricing](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/models/haiku-5-5/overview). Per-request tokens are an estimate from the prompt size; the live number will appear in HR Settings.\n\n## If Anthropic ships a competing product:\n\nA general assistant that answers California leave questions does not threaten Leave Ledger more than ChatGPT already does. What a model vendor cannot replicate without becoming an HR software company:\n1. **The employer's own data.** Hours, hire dates, 12-month leave history and sick balances per employee.\n2. **The deadline clock and the record.** Notices owed, when the clock started (including from a manager's flag), what was sent and when.\n3. **Role-based privacy.** Managers see dates only, employees see their own case, HR sees everything.\n4. **Distribution into payroll** (the M2 defense), once built.\n\nThe real vendor risk is not competition; it is a model change that quietly lowers intake accuracy. The golden set run on every model change is the defense.\n","03-the-margin/cost-curve.md":"# Cost Curve & Pricing Strategy\n\n> Week 3 · Nov 3, 2026 · Draft, for partner challenge. Builds on the Nov 2 product decisions: $3 per employee per month (PEPM), free for 1 to 4 employees, first segment 5 to 49 employees, owner is the buyer, design partners free for 3 months, Gusto first.\n\n**The short answer:** Clerk is not the margin problem. One intake costs about $0.0003, so a 20-employee company's whole month of Clerk use costs under one cent. What decides the margin is human support time and card fees, and both hit the smallest companies hardest. At 5 employees and 15 minutes of support a month, gross margin is about 14%.\n\n**How the numbers were built.** Every figure below is an estimate unless it cites a price page. The reference company has 20 employees and pays $60 a month. Assumptions, all to be replaced with real data:\n\n| Assumption | Value | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| Tokens per intake | 1,650 in, 250 out | The system prompt is 4,812 characters (about 1,200 tokens at 4 characters per token), plus a short message. Haiku 5.5's new tokenizer uses about 30% more tokens for the same text ([Mixed](https://mixed-news.com/en/?p=35955)), so 1,200 + 100 = 1,300, times 1.3 ≈ 1,650. Output is the JSON draft (about 150 to 250 tokens). HR Settings shows the real count. |\n| Clerk calls per company per month | 20 | 20 employees, about 5 short sick absences each a year plus 3 longer leaves ≈ 105 requests a year ≈ 9 a month, doubled for rewording, retries and HR reading manager notes. |\n| Support time per company per month | 15 minutes | No data yet. This is the number to pressure-test. |\n| Cost of an hour of support | $40 | A placeholder for Francis's time or a part-time helper. Change it and every margin below moves. |\n| Payment | Card, monthly | Stripe 2.9% + $0.30 per charge, plus Stripe Billing 0.7% ([NerdWallet](https://www.nerdwallet.com/business/software/learn/stripe-fees)). |\n\n## Cost Model\n\nPer company per month for the 20-employee reference company (PEPM in brackets = divide by 20). \"10 companies\" and \"100 companies\" show how fixed hosting spreads out.\n\n| Cost Category | Per-User/Month | Notes |\n|--------------|----------------|-------|\n| Inference (primary model) | $0.006 per company ($0.0003 PEPM) | Claude Haiku 5.5 at $0.10 per million input tokens and $0.50 per million output ([Mixed](https://mixed-news.com/en/?p=35955), [Haiku 5.5 API guide](https://dev.to/hassann/how-to-use-the-claude-haiku-55-api--1981)). 1,650 × $0.10/1M + 250 × $0.50/1M = $0.00029 per call, × 20 calls. The employee file column match (`netlify/lib/rostermap.mjs`) runs about once at setup and adds a fraction of a cent. |\n| Inference (cascading/triage) | $0 | No second model. Haiku is already the cheapest tier, and the rules engine (free) does all decisions. See Cascading Strategy. |\n| Infrastructure | $2.00 at 10 companies, $0.20 at 100 | Netlify Pro is $20 a month for 3,000 credits ([Netlify, Apr 2026](https://www.netlify.com/changelog/2026-04-14-pricing-updates-april-2026/)). Usage per company is small, so this is mostly a fixed cost shared out. |\n| Data/storage | about $0 to $0.20 | Netlify Blobs (cases, logs, 14 days of daily backups) sit inside the Netlify credits at this size. Email (Resend) is free up to 3,000 emails a month but capped at 100 a day; Pro is $20 for 50,000 ([Resend](https://www.resend.com/pricing.md)). Plan on Pro (another $0.20 per company at 100 companies) once letters and receipts go out for real. |\n| Human-in-the-loop | $10.00 | 15 minutes × $40 an hour. HR's own sign-off on each notice is the customer's time, not ours. This line is our support time: setup help, \"is this right?\" questions, design partner check-ins. |\n| Payments (not in the template, added) | $2.46 | $60 × 2.9% + $0.30 = $2.04, plus $60 × 0.7% = $0.42. |\n| **Total AI COGS** | **$0.006 per company** | AI is 0.04% of all costs. Total cost to serve is about $14.47 at 10 companies and $12.87 at 100. |\n\n**Gross margin by company size** (all estimates, card monthly, 15 minutes support):\n\n| Company | Revenue | Cost at 10 companies | Gross margin | Without support time |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 employees | $15 | $12.85 | **14%** | 81% |\n| 20 employees | $60 | $14.47 | **76%** (79% at 100 companies) | 93% |\n| 49 employees | $147 | $17.60 | **88%** (89% at 100 companies) | 95% |\n| 1 to 4 employees (free) | $0 | about $2 hosting share + any support | negative | |\n\nWhat this says: below about 10 employees, every support minute matters. A 5-employee company leaves $12.15 a month after payments, hosting and Clerk ($15 − $0.84 − $2.00 − $0.01). At $40 an hour that is about 18 minutes of support before the company costs money. A 20-employee company can absorb about 83 minutes. The free tier only works if it gets no human support at all.\n\n## Cascading Strategy\n<!-- Cheap model → frontier model routing logic -->\n\nThe template assumes a cheap model hands hard cases to an expensive one. Leave Ledger's cascade has a different first step: **no model at all**.\n\n**Triage model:** The rules engine and fixed letter templates (`public/engine.mjs`, `public/notices.mjs`), cost $0. They decide eligibility, pay estimates and deadlines, and write every notice. Notice drafting shipped on Nov 1 as fixed wording filled from the case, not AI.\n**Frontier model:** Claude Haiku 5.5 for reading free text (intake, manager notes, file columns). There is no bigger model in the path, and none is needed today.\n**Routing rule:** Form first; Clerk only when someone types in their own words. When Clerk is unsure (low confidence on reason), it does not escalate to a bigger model. It asks HR, because a person checking is the trust story (M4) and costs the customer seconds, not us money.\n**Expected cascade ratio:** 100% of AI calls on Haiku. If the 50-case golden set ever shows Haiku under 90% on leave type (kill criterion 4), the fallback is Sonnet 5.5 at $2 / $10 per million ([Mixed](https://mixed-news.com/en/?p=35955)): $0.0058 a call, 20 times Haiku. Even sending every call to Sonnet costs $0.12 a month for the reference company (0.2% of revenue). Escalating 10% of calls brings the blended cost to $0.00084 a call.\n\n**Not worth doing yet:**\n- **Prompt caching.** Haiku 5.5 can cache prompts of 512 tokens or more ([guide](https://dev.to/hassann/how-to-use-the-claude-haiku-55-api--1981)), and ours qualifies, but the cache lasts 5 minutes and a small company sends a request every few days. Cache writes cost 25% more than plain input, so it would raise cost. Revisit only for bulk work (the eval run, a large file import).\n- **The 100,000 token price cliff.** Above 100k tokens a prompt costs 5 times as much. Intake is about 1,650 tokens and the column match is capped at 80 columns, so we are far below it. Keep it in mind for future \"answer from the whole case history\" features.\n\n## Pricing Model\n\n**Current pricing:** $3 PEPM, free for 1 to 4 employees (decided Nov 2). Design partners free for 3 months. No billing built yet.\n**Proposed AI pricing:** No separate AI charge. Clerk is included and not metered. At $0.0003 PEPM, charging for it would cost more to explain than it earns. The daily limits already in the code (15 a user, 30 a workspace, 400 across the site) cap the worst case at about $0.26 a workspace a month and about $3.50 for the whole site.\n**Model:** seat-based (per employee per month). Usage-based and outcome-based pricing do not fit: the cost does not vary with usage, and an owner wants a predictable bill.\n\nTwo changes to how the seat price is collected, for Francis to decide (Week 3 questions):\n1. **Annual prepay, by bank transfer (ACH), as the default for small companies.** A 5-employee company paying $15 by card monthly loses 5.6% to fees ($0.84). Paying $180 a year by ACH costs $1.44 (0.8%, capped at $5) plus $1.26 Billing fee, 1.5% in total. It also cuts churn and invoice handling.\n2. **Self-serve support below 10 employees.** Email support only, with the Guide tab and walkthroughs doing the work. Setup calls only for 10 or more employees and design partners.\n\n**Competitor price check (Nov 2026):** Tilt now lists **Essentials at $2.50 PEPM**, with integrations +$0.60, premium support +$1.50 and Tilley phone intake +$0.50; Professional at $3.50 is \"coming soon\" ([Tilt pricing](https://hellotilt.com/pricing)). That is lower than the $4 to $6 in M2. A small company wanting Gusto sync would pay Tilt $3.10 PEPM, close to our $3 with Gusto included. The Oct 30 decision says \"our price and our notice clock are our edge.\" On list price alone the edge is now thin, so the case rests on the notice clock, California-only depth, self-serve setup in minutes (Tilt quotes 4 to 6 weeks of implementation under 500 employees), and no minimums. Draft, for partner challenge.\n\n## Stress Tests\n\nFor the 20-employee reference company at 10 companies (gross margin 75.9%).\n\n| Scenario | Impact on Margin | Response |\n|----------|-----------------|----------|\n| Inference costs 3x | Clerk goes from $0.006 to $0.017 a month. Margin moves by 0.02 points. | Nothing on price. Run the golden set on the cheaper option if one appears. |\n| Heaviest segment doubles | Clerk use doubling: under $0.01. **Support time doubling to 30 minutes: margin falls from 76% to 59%.** For a 5-employee company it goes negative. | Support time is the real heavy segment. Measure minutes per company from the first design partner. Self-serve below 10 employees; ship the plain-language answers (Clerk) so employees ask Clerk before they ask us. |\n| Model provider raises prices 50% | $0.003 more a month. No change. | Use it to finish the second-provider adapter (M2 kill switch), which is about portability, not cost. |\n| Tilt prices at $2.50 PEPM (happening now) | If we matched it: revenue $50 for 20 employees, margin falls to about 72%; a 5-employee company at $12.50 slips just below break-even with 15 minutes of support. | Do not match yet. Test $3 against Tilt's $2.50 + $0.60 integrations in the interviews (Week 3 question 1). |\n| Gusto takes a 20% revenue share (hypothetical) | Gusto's app partner terms are not public; this is a what-if. $12 off $60: margin falls to about 56%. | Ask about partner fees in the Gusto developer application before building the listing. If the share is real, a $3.50 price through Gusto may be needed. |\n| Card payments, monthly, for every company | Already in the base case (4.1% of revenue at 20 employees). | Annual ACH default saves about 3 points at 20 employees and 4 points at 5. |\n\n## Board One-Pager\n<!-- Before/After: Old SaaS revenue vs. AI usage revenue for your product -->\n\n**Before (traditional SaaS):** Leave Ledger without Clerk: $3 PEPM, rules engine, deadline clock, template letters. Cost to serve a 20-employee company: about $14.46 a month (payments $2.46, hosting $2.00, support $10.00). Gross margin about 76%.\n**After (AI-enabled):** Same price, Clerk included. AI adds $0.006 a month. Gross margin about 76%. The AI is a feature, not a cost line, and is not resold by usage.\n**Net margin shift:** −0.01 points from inference. The bigger upside is indirect: if Clerk intake and, later, Clerk answers cut support from 15 to 10 minutes a company a month, margin rises about 5.5 points (from 76% to 81%). That is the claim to prove with design partners, not to assume.\n\n**Break-even at:** Running costs once paying are about $40 a month (Netlify Pro $20, Resend Pro $20). Each paying 20-employee company contributes about $47.50 after payments, Clerk and 15 minutes of support, so **1 paying 20-employee company covers the running costs**. With only 5-employee companies it takes **10** ($4.16 each). One-time costs come on top: the lawyer review of rules, letters and the city sick leave table (no quote yet) and trademark clearance (usually a few hundred dollars, per the Nov 2 notes). Each $1,000 of one-time cost is about 21 months of one 20-employee company's contribution. The 3 free design partners cost about $58 a month each in support time (a weekly 20-minute check-in is about 87 minutes a month), about $520 in total over the 3 months. This does not count Francis's own time beyond support.\n\n## What would change these numbers\n\n1. **Real tokens per intake** from HR Settings (AI cost this month ÷ requests). The estimate could be off by 30% either way; it changes nothing at this size.\n2. **Real support minutes per company** from design partners. This one changes everything.\n3. **Gusto's partner terms** (fees or revenue share, if any).\n4. **Interview answers on price** against Tilt's $2.50.\n\n## Code notes (not changed; for Francis)\n\n- The site-wide limit of 400 Clerk calls a day (`LL_AI_DAY` in `netlify/lib/handler.mjs`) is fine now. At roughly 100 or more active companies it may start turning users away on busy days. It is an environment setting, so raise it in Netlify when needed.\n- HR Settings \"AI cost this month\" uses the list price table in `handler.mjs` ($0.10 / $0.50), which matches the current Haiku 5.5 price. The column match calls are counted in the same total, which is fine.\n- Billing is not built. Annual ACH would need Stripe Billing with bank debits enabled.\n","04-the-contract/golden-dataset.md":"# Golden Dataset & Reliability Contract\n\n## Golden Dataset Spec\n\n| # | Input | Expected Output | Edge Case? | Judge Type |\n|---|-------|----------------|-----------|-----------|\n| 1 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 2 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 3 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 4 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n| 5 | | | Y/N | rule / LLM |\n\n**Adversarial rows included:** __\n**Coverage gaps identified by partner:**\n\n## Confidence UX Design\n\n**Approach:** show uncertainty / tiered confidence / human-in-loop trigger\n\n**High confidence (>90%):**\n**Medium confidence (70-90%):**\n**Low confidence (<70%):**\n\n**User control surface:**\n\n## Reliability Contract\n\n| Metric | Target | Measurement | Alert Threshold |\n|--------|--------|-------------|-----------------|\n| Accuracy | | | |\n| Hallucination rate | | | |\n| Latency (p95) | | | |\n| Drift velocity | | | |\n\n## HITL Architecture\n<!-- When does a human step in? What's the escalation path? -->\n\n## Red-Team Findings\n*What failure mode did your partner find that you missed?*\n","05-the-guardrails/compounding-system.md":"# Compounding System Design\n\n## Feedback Loops\n\n| Loop | Input | Output | Compounds? | Status |\n|------|-------|--------|-----------|--------|\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n| | | | Y/N | active / broken / missing |\n\n**Broken loop identified by partner:**\n**Fix plan:**\n\n## Context Connectivity\n<!-- How does knowledge flow across teams and domains? Where does it silo? -->\n\n## Governance Policy\n\n**Scope:**\n**Autonomy boundaries:**\n**Escalation triggers:**\n**Audit cadence:**\n**Regulatory exposure (EU AI Act / other):**\n\n## Agent Topology\n<!-- If using agents: what can each agent do? What can't it do? Who approves what? -->\n\n## Shadow AI Audit\n\n| Tool | Owner | Risk Level | Decision |\n|------|-------|-----------|----------|\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n| | | H / M / L | keep / govern / kill |\n\n**Total tools found:**\n**Tools after triage:**\n**Estimated hidden spend:**\n","06-the-pitch/roadmap.md":"# Three-Horizon Roadmap & Board Pitch\n\n## Roadmap\n\n### Horizon 1 · Now (0-3 months)\n*Quick wins. Ship with existing capabilities.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n### Horizon 2 · Next (3-9 months)\n*Bets. Requires new capabilities or integrations.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n### Horizon 3 · Bet (9-18 months)\n*Moonshots. High uncertainty, high potential.*\n\n| Initiative | Metric | Confidence |\n|-----------|--------|-----------|\n| | | H / M / L |\n\n## Board Pitch\n\n**Thesis (1 sentence):**\n\n**The case:**\n1. Why now:\n2. What's defensible:\n3. The economics:\n\n**The risks:**\n1. Trust / failure modes:\n2. Scale / governance:\n3. Competitive:\n\n**The ask:**\n\n## M1 Baseline vs. Now\n*Your 3-sentence AI strategy from Module 1 vs. what you'd say now:*\n\n**M1 baseline:**\n\n**Now:**\n","README.md":"# Leave Ledger · AI Product Strategy\n\n> A living strategy built across 6 weekly sessions, using the Product School AI product strategy template. Each week adds one module. By week 6, this folder is the strategy: version-controlled and ready for a board.\n\n**The bet:** California leave of absence management for employers with 5 to 200 employees.\n**Last updated:** Nov 3, 2026 (Week 3)\n**Customer interviews:** 0 of 10 (kill criteria check on Nov 23, 2026)\n**Partner challenges done:** 0 of 6\n\n---\n\n## Strategy at a Glance\n\n| Component | Module | Week of | Status | Key Artifact |\n|-----------|--------|---------|--------|-------------|\n| **The Bet** | M1 | Oct 21 | [x] Draft, needs partner challenge | `01-the-bet/` |\n| **The Moat** | M2 | Oct 28 | [x] Draft, needs partner challenge | `02-the-moat/` |\n| **The Margin** | M3 | Nov 4 | [x] Draft, needs partner challenge | `03-the-margin/` |\n| **The Contract** | M4 | Nov 11 | [ ] | `04-the-contract/` |\n| **The Guardrails** | M5 | Nov 18 | [ ] | `05-the-guardrails/` |\n| **The Pitch** | M6 | Nov 25 | [ ] | `06-the-pitch/` |\n\n---\n\n## The Bet (M1)\n\n**What we're building, for whom, why now.**\n\n- **Product:** Leave Ledger. Works out which California and federal leaves an employee qualifies for, what the state pays, and the notices and deadlines the employer owes. Separate views for HR, employee and manager.\n- **AI Value Archetype:** Copilot (confirmed Oct 26). AI handles intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; a deterministic rules engine decides eligibility; a human signs off on every notice.\n- **Vulnerability Scores:** Moat 2/5 · Data 1/5 · Platform 4/5 (exposed)\n- **Top Risk:** Distribution. Small businesses buy HR through their payroll provider. If Gusto or Rippling ships California leave first, a standalone tool has no way in.\n- **Confidence:** L (until 10 customer interviews)\n- **Prototype:** Netlify test site, 10 test accounts (link in `01-the-bet/prototype.md`)\n- **Kill Criteria:** Under 3 of 10 interviewees feel the pain, or under 2 of 10 would pay $3 per employee per month (our price), by Nov 23; or a payroll platform ships California leave stacking before we have 5 paying employers.\n- **Changed by Nov 2 product decisions:** first segment narrowed to 5 to 49 employees (state leaves only, no FMLA), owner is the buyer, price set at $3 per employee per month (free for 1 to 4). Kill criterion 2 changed from $4 to $3 on Nov 3 to test the price we will charge (Week 3 question 2).\n\n→ Details: [`01-the-bet/`](01-the-bet/)\n\n---\n\n## The Moat (M2) · Week of Oct 28\n\n**Why this won't get copied in 6 months.**\n\n- **Data Flywheel Score:** 6/20 (Correction 2 · Preference 1 · Domain Context 2 · Network 1)\n- **Weakest Loop:** Network. Gusto, Tilt and Paylocity see far more leaves than we will for years. Fix: don't compete there; build Correction (weekly golden-set review of Clerk fixes) and Preference (company policy profile) first; add de-identified California benchmarks only after 25+ companies, opt-in, never below 10 pooled companies.\n- **Competitive Position:** Axes are California depth and fit for a 5 to 49 employee business. Leave Ledger targets high on both. Paylocity+Aidora and Mitratech are deep but enterprise; Gusto fits small business but is shallow on California leave; Tilt is closest (AI phone intake, Gusto and Rippling integrations, $4 to $6 per employee per month).\n- **Encroachment Defense:** Join Gusto rather than fight it (app directory listing with two-way sync); own the dispute-ready record (notice clock, signed notices, 12-month history); publish rules-engine tests and Clerk accuracy score; sell through bookkeepers, PEOs and employment lawyers.\n- **Decided Oct 30 and Nov 2:** Gusto is a partner and the first payroll connection; price and the notice clock are the claimed edge over Tilt. Tilt now lists $2.50 per employee per month (Nov 2026), so the price edge needs re-testing (see M3).\n- **Vendor Portability:** Partial. Clerk uses only Anthropic's Claude, and the code calling it is written for Anthropic alone. If Anthropic raised prices or went down, the rest of the product (eligibility, deadlines, notices) keeps working, but intake would fall back to the manual form. Fix, about 2 days of work: make that one piece of code able to call a second AI provider, then check the second provider gets the 20 test requests right before relying on it.\n\n→ Details: [`02-the-moat/`](02-the-moat/)\n\n---\n\n## The Margin (M3) · Week of Nov 4\n\n**Will this make money or bleed it?**\n\n- **Gross Margin (current):** About 76% for a 20-employee company at $3 per employee per month ($60), with 10 companies on the platform (79% at 100). 14% at 5 employees, 88% at 49. Estimates; support time (15 minutes a month at $40 an hour, assumed) is the biggest cost, then card fees.\n- **Gross Margin (AI-adjusted):** The same. One Clerk intake costs about $0.0003 (Haiku 5.5 at $0.10 / $0.50 per million tokens, about 1,650 in and 250 out), so a month of Clerk for 20 employees is under one cent. AI moves margin by 0.01 points. Upside if Clerk cuts support by 5 minutes a company a month: about 81%.\n- **Pricing Model:** Seat-based, $3 per employee per month, free for 1 to 4 (decided Nov 2). Clerk included, not metered. Proposed: annual prepay by bank transfer and email-only support below 10 employees. Tilt now lists $2.50 (plus $0.60 for integrations), so price alone is a thin edge.\n- **Cascading Strategy:** Rules engine and template letters first (free, no model); Haiku 5.5 only for free text; when unsure, ask HR rather than a bigger model. Sonnet 5.5 is the fallback only if Haiku fails the 90% leave type bar, and would still cost $0.12 a month per company.\n- **Break-even at:** Running costs about $40 a month (Netlify Pro, Resend Pro). 1 paying 20-employee company, or 10 paying 5-employee companies, covers them. One-time lawyer review (no quote yet) and trademark clearance come on top: each $1,000 is about 21 company-months at 20 employees. Draft, for partner challenge.\n\n→ Details: [`03-the-margin/`](03-the-margin/)\n\n---\n\n## The Contract (M4) · Week of Nov 11\n\n**Why users will trust a probabilistic system.**\n\n- **Reliability Target:**\n- **Golden Dataset:** __ rows, __ adversarial\n- **Confidence UX:** [approach]\n- **HITL Architecture:**\n- **Failure Mode Coverage:**\n\n→ Details: [`04-the-contract/`](04-the-contract/)\n\n---\n\n## The Guardrails (M5) · Week of Nov 18\n\n**What breaks when this scales, and what compounds.**\n\n- **Compounding System:** [describe feedback loops]\n- **Governance Posture:** [approach]\n- **Shadow AI Status:** __ tools found, __ triaged\n- **Agent Boundaries:**\n- **Regulatory Exposure:**\n\n→ Details: [`05-the-guardrails/`](05-the-guardrails/)\n\n---\n\n## The Pitch (M6) · Week of Nov 25\n\n**How you get this funded, shipped, and adopted.**\n\n- **Horizon 1 (Now):**\n- **Horizon 2 (Next):**\n- **Horizon 3 (Bet):**\n- **Board Narrative:** [1-sentence thesis]\n- **Key Metric:**\n\n→ Details: [`06-the-pitch/`](06-the-pitch/)\n\n---\n\n## Open decisions (Week 3)\n\n1. **Tilt's $2.50 price.** Tilt now lists Essentials at $2.50 per employee per month (plus $0.60 for Gusto and Rippling sync), below our $3. Keep $3 and sell the notice clock and setup in minutes, or change the price? Either way, ask each interviewee what they would pay with Tilt's list price in front of them.\n2. **Kill criterion 2 price.** Criterion 2 still asks whether 2 of 10 would pay at least $4 per employee per month, set when Tilt's lowest tier looked like $4. Our price is now $3 and Tilt's is $2.50. Change the bar to $3 (our price), keep $4, or something else?\n3. **Small companies: billing and support.** At 5 employees and 15 minutes of support a month, gross margin is about 14% (18 minutes and the company costs money). Make annual prepay by bank transfer the default and support email-only below 10 employees, set a monthly minimum, or leave it until design partners show real support time?\n4. **Clerk's first numbers.** Week 2's \"turn on Clerk\" is done, but no score is recorded yet. Add the golden set result (inbox, Clerk test run: % right on leave type, out of 50 cases) and HR Settings' requests and AI cost this month to `02-the-moat/kill-switch.md`, or paste them in this answer. They replace this week's token estimate and set the Week 4 baseline.\n\n## Changelog\n\n| Week | Date | What changed |\n|------|------|-------------|\n| 1 | Oct 26, 2026 | M1 drafted: market sizing, three-axis diagnostic, prototype bet, kill criteria |\n| 1 | Oct 27, 2026 | Copilot archetype confirmed; interview and partner challenge counters added for the progress page |\n| 1 | Oct 27, 2026 | Clerk intake built (Claude Haiku 5.5): own-words requests fill the form, corrections logged, 20-case golden set added |\n| 2 | Oct 28, 2026 | M2 drafted: flywheel 6/20, competitive map, encroachment threats (Gusto, Tilt, CalChamber), 90-day attack and defense, kill switch audit (portability Partial). Schedule moved to Wednesday sessions |\n| 3 | Nov 3, 2026 | M3 drafted: cost model (Clerk about $0.0003 an intake, gross margin about 76% at 20 employees, 14% at 5), no-model-first cascade, pricing on the Nov 2 decisions, stress tests incl. Tilt at $2.50, break-even. M1 and M2 summaries note the Nov 2 decisions and Tilt's new price |\n","decisions.md":"# Strategy decisions\n\n> Answered on the /strategy page of the test site and copied here automatically. Newest week first. Edit them on the page, not in this file; the page overwrites it.\n\n## Week 3 · The Margin\n\n### 1. Tilt's $2.50 price\n\n**Question:** Tilt now lists Essentials at $2.50 per employee per month (plus $0.60 for Gusto and Rippling sync), below our $3. Keep $3 and sell the notice clock and setup in minutes, or change the price? Either way, ask each interviewee what they would pay with Tilt's list price in front of them.\n\n**Status:** Decided · Nov 3, 2026 · Site admin\n\n**Answer:** Keep the same price as Tilt.  We compete on both price and value.\n\n### 3. Small companies: billing and support\n\n**Question:** At 5 employees and 15 minutes of support a month, gross margin is about 14% (18 minutes and the company costs money). Make annual prepay by bank transfer the default and support email-only below 10 employees, set a monthly minimum, or leave it until design partners show real support time?\n\n**Status:** Decided · Nov 3, 2026 · Site admin\n\n**Answer:** Make it the default.   We need to build the trust from our customers to have automated prepay.\n\n## Week 2 · The Moat\n\n### 1. Partner challenge on Tilt\n\n**Question:** Is Tilt the real threat rather than Gusto? It already has AI intake (Tilley), Gusto and Rippling sync, and California coverage. If yes, what does Leave Ledger do that Tilt will not do for a 15-person company?\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 30, 2026 · Site admin\n\n**Answer:** Our price and our notice clock is our edge.   To be tested in the interviews.\n\n### 2. Gusto: partner or competitor?\n\n**Question:** The defense assumes a Gusto app directory listing. Is that the distribution bet, or should the first channel be bookkeepers and PEOs?\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 30, 2026 · Site admin\n\n**Answer:** Gusto is a partner.  Without an established distribution network, reaching tiny companies will be cost prohibitive.\n\n### 3. Turn on the Copilot\n\n**Question:** Add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in Netlify and run `scripts/eval-intake.mjs`. The first accuracy score goes into `02-the-moat/kill-switch.md` and feeds Week 4.\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 30, 2026 · Site admin\n\n**Answer:** Done.\n\n### 4. Interviews\n\n**Question:** Kill criteria check is Nov 23. Ask each interviewee what they use for payroll; if most say Gusto, that confirms decision 2.\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 30, 2026 · Site admin\n\n**Answer:** Understood.  Will do.\n\n## Week 1 · The Bet\n\n### 1. Confirm the archetype\n\n**Question:** Copilot with a rules engine underneath, or something else?\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 28, 2026 · Grace Okafor\n\n**Answer:** Go with a Copilot concept.  This AI does intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; the rules engine alone decides eligibility, and a human (persona) signs off on every notice.\n\n<details><summary>Earlier answers (4)</summary>\n\n- Oct 28, 2026 · Decided: Go with a Copilot concept.  This AI does intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; the rules engine alone decides eligibility, and a human (persona-based) signs off on every notice.\n- Oct 28, 2026 · Decided: Go with a Copilot concept.  This AI does intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; the rules engine alone decides eligibility, and a human signs off on every notice.\n- Oct 28, 2026 · Decided: Go with a Copilot concept.  This AI does intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; the rules engine alone decides eligibility, and a human signs off on every notice.\n- Oct 26, 2026 · Decided: Go with Copilot. AI does intake, plain-language answers and notice drafts; the rules engine alone decides eligibility, and a human signs off on every notice.\n\n</details>\n\n### 2. Partner challenge\n\n**Question:** Test the three named attackers (Tilt, ChatGPT, Gusto) and the 1/5 data score with your course partner.\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 26, 2026 · Francis (in chat)\n\n**Answer:** Yes. Test Tilt, ChatGPT and Gusto as attackers, and the 1/5 data score, with my course partner.\n\n### 3. Prototype link\n\n**Question:** Add the Netlify URL to 01-the-bet/prototype.md.\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 26, 2026 · Francis (in chat)\n\n**Answer:** Yes. Done: https://spectacular-mousse-c413d6.netlify.app is in prototype.md.\n\n### 4. Start interviews\n\n**Question:** Kill criteria 1 and 2 need 10 conversations by Nov 23.\n\n**Status:** Decided · Oct 26, 2026 · Francis (in chat)\n\n**Answer:** Will do: 10 interviews with California office managers or HR generalists by Nov 23.\n","product-decisions.md":"# Product decisions\n\n> Decisions about the product and the business, made outside the course schedule. Newest first. Decided by Francis on Nov 2, 2026 unless noted.\n\n## Decided\n\n| # | Decision | What we chose | What it means in the product |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Legal sign-off | HR signs off on each leave, then can send a copy of the decision to legal. | Approve on each leave. \"Send a copy to legal\" emails the legal contact from Settings a summary: dates, each eligibility test and its result, letters and their delivery record. |\n| 2 | City sick leave laws | Cover the largest cities now. | Settings, \"City where most employees work\": San Francisco, Oakland, Berkeley, Emeryville, Los Angeles city and county, San Diego, Santa Monica. The employee gets the more generous of the state and the city rule. Needs the lawyer's check (see 1). |\n| 3 | Real employee data | A design partner bar for 2 or 3 companies. | The site admin switches on \"Design partner\" per workspace in the inbox, only after the checklist below is done. |\n| 4 | Employee words and AI | Keep Clerk, with an AI agreement that does not keep or train on the data, and a consent line employees see. | In design partner workspaces Clerk stays off until `LL_AI_REAL_DATA_OK=yes` is set in Netlify. The request form says what Clerk does with their words. |\n| 5 | Employer | A personal side venture with no connection to Francis's employer. | Nothing to build. |\n| 6 | Name | Keep \"Leave Ledger\" for now; clear it properly before spending on brand. | See trademark notes below. |\n| 7 | First segment | California employers with 5 to 49 employees. | State leaves only (no federal FMLA). This is where the California-only complexity is the edge. |\n| 8 | Price | $3 per employee per month. Free for 1 to 4 employees (decided Nov 2). | Companies under 5 owe only sick leave, so the free tier does not overlap the 5 to 49 target. No billing built yet. |\n| 9 | Buyer | The owner. | Pitch and onboarding speak to the owner: risk, time saved, cost. HR or the office manager runs it day to day. |\n| 10 | Pilot | Design partners, free for 3 months. | In return: a weekly 20-minute check-in and permission to quote them. |\n| 11 | Payroll connection | Gusto first. | Next: apply to Gusto's developer and app partner program. File import covers everyone else until then. |\n| 12 | Proof of delivery | Track it for every letter and show it. | Letters are sent through Leave Ledger (and emailed when set up). The employee opens and confirms receipt. HR sees the record on the leave, with times, and can print it. |\n| 13 | Where | California only. | No other states until after the first paying customers. |\n| 14 | AI assistant connection (MCP server) | Later. | Not built. Revisit after design partners. |\n\n## Design partner checklist\n\nSwitch a workspace to \"Design partner\" in the inbox only when every line is done for that company.\n\n- [ ] Signed design partner agreement: 3 months free, confidentiality, who owns the data (the company), deletion when the pilot ends, not legal advice.\n- [ ] Data processing terms signed with the company.\n- [ ] Privacy policy updated for real data, naming every outside service: hosting and storage (Netlify), AI (Anthropic), email (Resend), sign-in (Google).\n- [x] `LL_AI_REAL_DATA_OK=yes` set in Netlify (Nov 2). Keep the AI agreement on file: employee words are not kept or used for training.\n- [ ] Lawyer review of the rules, the letter templates and the city sick leave table done, and fixes made.\n- [ ] Company accounts only switched on for the workspace (Users tab).\n- [x] Export and delete for a workspace built and tested (Settings, Your data). Deleting removes data, users and backups.\n- [x] Daily backup of every workspace, 14 days kept, restore from the admin inbox. Backups live with the app's storage: they cover mistakes, not a hosting outage.\n- [ ] A short plan for a data breach: who is told, how fast (California Civil Code 1798.82).\n\n## Trademark notes (Nov 2, 2026)\n\nA web search found no product or registered mark called \"Leave Ledger\". Two things to know:\n\n- \"Leave ledger\" is ordinary HR wording. Frappe HR, an open source HR system, has a \"Leave Ledger\" report and \"Leave Ledger Entry\" records. A descriptive name like this is harder to register and to defend.\n- \"Ledger\" alone is a crowded mark, held by the crypto wallet company Ledger for software and finance. Different goods and services, but expect pushback if the filing leans on \"Ledger\".\n\nThis was a web search, not a USPTO search. Before spending on brand: run the USPTO trademark search for \"Leave Ledger\" and close variants in classes 9, 35 and 42, then have a trademark attorney do a clearance search (usually a few hundred dollars).\n","questions.md":"# Weekly decision questions\n\n> Every week's open decisions, kept here so earlier weeks stay answerable on the /strategy page. Each numbered item becomes an answer form there. Format: `1. **Short title.** The question.` The current week also appears under \"Open decisions\" in README.md.\n\n## Week 1 · The Bet\n\n1. **Confirm the archetype.** Copilot with a rules engine underneath, or something else?\n2. **Partner challenge.** Test the three named attackers (Tilt, ChatGPT, Gusto) and the 1/5 data score with your course partner. Record what they push on in `01-the-bet/diagnostic.md`.\n3. **Prototype link.** Add the Netlify URL to `01-the-bet/prototype.md`.\n4. **Start interviews.** Kill criteria 1 and 2 need 10 conversations by Nov 23. If you add 2 to 3 a week, they also feed M2 (data) and M3 (pricing).\n\n## Week 2 · The Moat\n\n1. **Partner challenge on Tilt.** Is Tilt the real threat rather than Gusto? It already has AI intake (Tilley), Gusto and Rippling sync, and California coverage. If yes, what does Leave Ledger do that Tilt will not do for a 15-person company?\n2. **Gusto: partner or competitor?** The defense assumes a Gusto app directory listing. Is that the distribution bet, or should the first channel be bookkeepers and PEOs?\n3. **Turn on the Copilot.** Add `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in Netlify and run `scripts/eval-intake.mjs`. The first accuracy score goes into `02-the-moat/kill-switch.md` and feeds Week 4.\n4. **Interviews.** Kill criteria check is Nov 23. Ask each interviewee what they use for payroll; if most say Gusto, that confirms decision 2.\n\n## Week 3 · The Margin\n\n1. **Tilt's $2.50 price.** Tilt now lists Essentials at $2.50 per employee per month (plus $0.60 for Gusto and Rippling sync), below our $3. Keep $3 and sell the notice clock and setup in minutes, or change the price? Either way, ask each interviewee what they would pay with Tilt's list price in front of them.\n2. **Kill criterion 2 price.** Criterion 2 still asks whether 2 of 10 would pay at least $4 per employee per month, set when Tilt's lowest tier looked like $4. Our price is now $3 and Tilt's is $2.50. Change the bar to $3 (our price), keep $4, or something else?\n3. **Small companies: billing and support.** At 5 employees and 15 minutes of support a month, gross margin is about 14% (18 minutes and the company costs money). Make annual prepay by bank transfer the default and support email-only below 10 employees, set a monthly minimum, or leave it until design partners show real support time?\n4. **Clerk's first numbers.** Week 2's \"turn on Clerk\" is done, but no score is recorded yet. Add the golden set result (inbox, Clerk test run: % right on leave type, out of 50 cases) and HR Settings' requests and AI cost this month to `02-the-moat/kill-switch.md`, or paste them in this answer. They replace this week's token estimate and set the Week 4 baseline.\n"}};
